schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Why Local Matters"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    Argues that place-based nodes do work global protocols structurally
    cannot: capital is light and can flow anywhere, but trust is heavy and
    must be built face-to-face over time (the "Ethereum Localism" framing:
    "what is heavy should be local, what is light should be global and
    shared" — i.e. subsidiarity). Covers what a node does that a protocol
    can't (onboarding, deciding which local projects are real, custody/
    accountability, the "eight forms of capital" beyond money), the standing
    network infrastructure a new node can plug into (Greenpill Network,
    quadratic funding via Gitcoin Grants), the "start small" pattern against
    building something nobody uses, designing against founder burnout from day
    one, and concrete tools a node can borrow (Mento/Celo local stablecoins,
    impactMarket UBI rails, the Regen Coordination Local ReFi Toolkit, BioFi
    Project templates).
  audience: people who already know web3 and are ready to stand up a local node
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Local autonomy vs. central overreach — the article frames subsidiarity
      and distributed local ownership as the defence against both a global
      DAO/funder steering local work and against founder burnout.
    - >-
      Starting small vs. building infrastructure — explicitly warns against
      setting up a DAO/token/five-tier governance model before the group has
      the coordination problems those solve.
  related_concepts:
    - subsidiarity
    - eight-forms-of-capital
  related_resources:
    - greenpill-network
    - mento
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/why-local-matters.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
# - Did not create a separate resource card for impactMarket or the BioFi Project individually —
#   both are named "borrow, don't build" infrastructure examples in a single closing list, not
#   independently developed; captured Mento/Celo as the single representative example since it's
#   discussed with the most named detail (specific stablecoins: cKES, cREAL, cCOP, PUSO).
